<div class="page">
  <h3 style="margin-top: 30px;">Web Resources</h3>

  <p>
    <span data-field="resource_title_1">{{ resource_title_1 }}</span><br>
    Mississippi State University Extension Service, IS 1500<br>
    <a href="{{ resource_url_1 }}">{{ resource_url_1 }}</a><br>
    Flame weeding has a long history of use in the Mississippi Delta states. This fact sheet from Mississippi State University provides a brief introduction and summary on flame cultivation for cotton.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span data-field="resource_title_2">{{ resource_title_2 }}</span><br>
    <a href="{{ resource_url_2 }}">{{ resource_url_2 }}</a><br>
    The Agricultural Flaming Guide provides a history of flame cultivation, with a summary of methods and flaming techniques for corn, soybeans, grain sorghum, cotton, potatoes, tomatoes, cole crops, alfalfa, and grape vineyards.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span data-field="resource_title_3">{{ resource_title_3 }}</span><br>
    Sustainable Practices for Vegetable Production in the South<br>
    Dr. Mary Peet, NCSU<br>
    <a href="{{ resource_url_3 }}">{{ resource_url_3 }}</a><br>
    Dr. Mary Peet published one of the very first books on sustainable vegetable production. This section touches on flame weeding, with a couple of farmer profiles.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span data-field="resource_title_4">{{ resource_title_4 }}</span><br>
    From: <span data-field="resource_source_4">{{ resource_source_4 }}</span><br>
    <a href="{{ resource_url_4 }}">{{ resource_url_4 }}</a><br>
    A section on flaming from Steel in the Field, a publication from SANS (Sustainable Agriculture Network). Steel in the Field is a practical handbook on non-chemical weed control, with very helpful diagrams and descriptions of 37 specialized cultivators used in mechanical weed control; highly recommended for the organic farmer's bookshelf.
  </p>

  <p>
    <span data-field="resource_title_5">{{ resource_title_5 }}</span><br>
